WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.020
A cochlear implant gives many adults with severe hearing loss useful hearing.

2
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.880
The same idea in the eye often gives streaks, not pixels.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.800
Why?

4
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:18.813
The implant skips damaged hair cells and excites the auditory nerve along the

5
00:00:18.813 --> 00:00:23.033
cochlea's map. High frequencies go to the base, low toward the apex.

6
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:26.117
A contact is hardware on the array.

7
00:00:27.037 --> 00:00:28.597
A channel is a band of frequencies.

8
00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:32.357
Truly separate channels need separate groups of neurons.

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:38.430
Predict. Take 22 contacts, spanning about 16 mm.

10
00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.790
Each contact's current spreads through fluid and bone.

11
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.370
How many non-overlapping stretches fit along the array?

12
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:53.543
About nine, far fewer than 22.

13
00:00:54.543 --> 00:00:57.343
Neighbouring fields overlap and excite the same neurons.

14
00:00:58.263 --> 00:00:59.723
This is called channel interaction.

15
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:05.463
The spread was picked to land near real data, so this illustrates the idea rather

16
00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:11.043
than testing it. In 18 implant users, speech recognition plateaued at 8 to 10

17
00:01:11.043 --> 00:01:12.043
channels.

18
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.927
Channel interaction is one main reason.

19
00:01:16.087 --> 00:01:19.007
Neural survival and how far the array reaches matter too.

20
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.400
Here is the one idea.

21
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.920
A prosthesis delivers detail only as far as it drives distinct groups of neurons.

22
00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:31.313
So a count of contacts is not a count of channels.

23
00:01:32.153 --> 00:01:35.633
That has to be measured, by testing what the listener can tell apart.

24
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:42.613
Test it. Some arrays are precurved, shaped to hug the inner wall, close to the nerve

25
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:44.353
cell bodies in the cochlea's core.

26
00:01:45.366 --> 00:01:50.646
Predict. If spread sets the limit, what should a well-placed precurved array allow?

27
00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.076
More usable channels.

28
00:01:58.956 --> 00:02:03.216
In an earlier cohort from the same group, those listeners kept improving past 10.

29
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.750
The plateau group had straight arrays, or precurved arrays that crossed into the

30
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.210
upper chamber. Those generally sat farther from the nerve.

31
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.400
So there is no single channel number.

32
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.880
It depends on the listener, the placement and the test.

33
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:28.243
None of this means the implant works poorly.

34
00:02:29.163 --> 00:02:33.583
In 96 adults, word scores rose by 40.5 percentage points.

35
00:02:34.323 --> 00:02:37.523
That was with the implant plus a hearing aid in the other ear.

36
00:02:38.516 --> 00:02:43.616
So ask how distinctly a device drives separate groups of neurons, and test what the

37
00:02:43.616 --> 00:02:44.696
listener can tell apart.
